<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Livy</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">c. 59 BCE – c. 17 CE</span> · <a href="/centuries/1st-century-bce">1st century BCE</a> · Also known as Titus Livius</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Livy (Titus Livius) was a Roman historian from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/padua">Patavium</a> (Padua) who wrote Ab Urbe Condita, a history of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rome">Rome</a> from its foundation in 142 books, of which 35 survive. Writing under Augustus, he is a principal source for early Rome, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/punic-wars">Punic Wars</a> and Rome's wars with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gauls">Gauls</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/greeks">Greeks</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/iberians">Iberians</a>, although he relied on earlier annalists and used the past to draw moral lessons.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/livy.jpg" alt="Livy" width="320" height="453" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Acediscovery · CC BY 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Titus-Livius-Austrian-Parliament-Building.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
